      "layout": "title-left-right",
      "title": "",
      "subtitle": "",
      "duration_frames": 1032,
      "transition": "slideUp",
      "narration": "Two ideas make Dijkstra work. First, optimal substructure: if the shortest path from source to node v passes through u, then the piece from source to u must itself be shortest — otherwise you could splice in a shorter prefix and beat the supposedly-optimal path. Second, edge relaxation: we keep a tentative distance dist[v] and repeatedly ask whether going through u gives a shorter route. Relaxation is the atomic operation Dijkstra performs thousands of times; the entire algorithm is just relaxation applied in a clever order.",
      "panels": [
        {
          "area": "left",
          "type": "MathFormula",
          "size_ratio": 1,
          "delay_frames": 0,
          "data": {
