      "duration_frames": 972,
      "transition": "slideLeft",
      "narration": "Here is the greedy heart of the algorithm. Keep every node's tentative distance in a priority queue. Repeatedly extract the node with the smallest tentative distance — call it u — and declare its distance final. Then relax every edge leaving u, updating its neighbors' tentative distances. The gamble is that the closest unsettled node's distance can never improve, so we lock it in immediately. That single greedy commitment is what makes the algorithm fast and, as we'll prove next, correct.",
