    {
      "id": "scene-5",
      "layout": "title-content",
      "title": "",
      "subtitle": "",
      "duration_frames": 1020,
      "transition": "slideLeft",
      "narration": "Why is it safe to freeze u's distance the moment we extract it? Suppose, for contradiction, that a shorter path to u existed. That path must leave the settled set at some edge into an unsettled node y. Because all edge weights are non-negative, y's tentative distance is already at least as small as u's, and the remaining path only adds more weight. So the alternative path cannot be shorter than dist[u] — a contradiction. This exchange argument is exactly why non-negative weights are non-negotiable.",
      "panels": [
        {
          "area": "main",
          "type": "EquationDerivation",
          "size_ratio": 1,
          "delay_frames": 0,
          "data": {
            "title": "Correctness by contradiction",
            "steps": [
              {
                "label": "Assume a shorter path to u exists",
                "tokens": [
