    {
      "id": "scene-6",
      "layout": "title-left-right",
      "title": "",
      "subtitle": "",
      "duration_frames": 1020,
      "transition": "fade",
      "narration": "The priority queue you choose dictates the complexity. With a binary heap — Python's heapq, or std::priority_queue — each of the V extract-min and E decrease-key operations costs O(log V), giving O((V+E) log V). A Fibonacci heap drops decrease-key to amortized O(1), improving the bound to O(E + V log V), though its large constants make it slower in practice on real graphs. The common production trick, shown here, is a lazy binary heap: never decrease-key, just push duplicates and skip stale entries on pop.",
      "panels": [
        {
          "area": "left",
          "type": "CodeBlock",
          "size_ratio": 2,
          "delay_frames": 0,
          "data": {
            "title": "Lazy-deletion Dijkstra (Python)",
            "code": "import heapq\n\ndef dijkstra(graph, source):\n    dist = {v: float('inf') for v in graph}\n    dist[source] = 0\n    pq = [(0, source)]          # (distance, node)\n    while pq:\n        d, u = heapq.heappop(pq)\n        if d > dist[u]:\n            continue            # stale entry, skip\n        for v, w in graph[u]:\n            nd = d + w\n            if nd < dist[v]:\n                dist[v] = nd\n                heapq.heappush(pq, (nd, v))\n    return dist",
            "language": "python",
            "accentColor": "#4ade80",
            "highlightLines": [
              9,
              10
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "area": "right",
          "type": "BulletList",
          "size_ratio": 1,
          "delay_frames": 0,
          "data": {
            "title": "Complexity by data structure",
            "items": [
              "Array scan for min — O(V²), best on dense graphs where E ≈ V²",
              "Binary heap — O((V+E) log V), the everyday default",
              "Fibonacci heap — O(E + V log V) amortized, big constants, rarely faster in practice",
              "Lazy heap — pushes stale duplicates, bounded by O(E) extra entries",
              "Radix / bucket queue (Dial's) — O(E + V·C) when weights are small integers"
            ],
            "accentColor": "#a78bfa"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "scene-7",
      "layout": "left-right",
      "title": "",
      "subtitle": "",
      "duration_frames": 936,
      "transition": "slideUp",
      "narration": "Dijkstra's Achilles heel is negative edge weights. A negative edge can retroactively make a path to an already-settled node cheaper, violating the freeze-once invariant and producing wrong answers — with a negative cycle, no shortest path even exists. This is a real constraint: currency-arbitrage graphs and some cost models have negative edges, and there you must switch to Bellman-Ford or Johnson's algorithm. Dijkstra also computes single-source paths; for all-pairs on dense graphs, Floyd-Warshall is often the better tool.",
