      "subtitle": "",
      "duration_frames": 1152,
      "transition": "zoom",
      "narration": "In practice, raw Dijkstra is too slow for continental road networks with tens of millions of nodes, so real routing engines layer on speedups. A* adds an admissible heuristic — straight-line distance — to steer the search toward the goal, and bidirectional search runs two frontiers that meet in the middle, roughly halving the explored area. Google Maps and OSRM go further with Contraction Hierarchies, precomputing shortcut edges so a query touches only a tiny fraction of the graph, answering continental routes in under a millisecond. All of these still have Dijkstra's relaxation at their core.",
