      "duration_frames": 924,
      "transition": "none",
      "narration": "Dijkstra is greedy done right: settle the nearest unsettled node, relax its edges, repeat — and an exchange argument proves that greedy commitment is always optimal, as long as weights stay non-negative. Remember the three levers: the invariant that settled means final, the non-negative-weight requirement that makes it true, and the priority queue that sets your complexity. Master those and you understand not just Dijkstra, but the whole family of shortest-path algorithms built on top of it.",
